{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_72","type":"reasoning","text":"Non-PIE executable (fixed base 0x400000). GNU_STACK RW (non-executable, NX). GNU_RELRO present but need BIND_NOW check - no BIND_NOW in dyn output shown, so partial RELRO. \n\nSo full RELRO not present → GOT writable. Non-PIE → fixed addresses.\n\nNow let's understand the overflow better. Let me look at fr_dns_label_to_value_box carefully and figure out the actual overflow. Actually, wait. Let me reconsider the whole thing. The \"known vulnerability\" is described as dns_label_decode lacks validation. The crash is a read. But how do we get from a read overflow to an OOB write?\n\nLet me re-read fr_dns_label_to_value_box:\n\n```\nslen = fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length(packet, src, len, &after, lb);   // after = next label pointer\nif (slen <= 0) return slen;\n\nif (slen == 1) { ... return after - label; }\n\nfr_value_box_bstr_alloc(ctx, &q, dst, NULL, slen, tainted);   // allocate slen bytes\ncurrent = label;\np = (uint8_t *)q;\nq += slen;   // q = end of buffer\n\nwhile ((current < after) && (*current != 0x00)) {\n    slen = dns_label_decode(packet, &current, &next);\n    if ((p + slen) > (uint8_t *) q) { fail; }\n    if (p != dst->vb_strvalue) *(p++) = '.';\n    memcpy(p, current + 1, slen);   // <-- copy slen bytes\n    p += slen;\n    current = next;\n}\nif (p != q) fail;\n*p = '\\0';\n```\n\nSo buffer size = slen (uncompressed length including dots, from fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length). The check `(p + slen) > q` happens BEFORE the dot. If p == q-1 (one byte left), p+slen where slen=1 → p+1 = q, not > q, passes. Then dot written at p (q-1), p becomes q. Then memcpy(p, current+1, 1) writes 1 byte at q, which is 1 past the buffer! And then p += 1 → p = q+1. Then loop condition: current=next. If next >= after or *current==0, loop ends. Then `p != q` → fail (since p=q+1). But the 1-byte OOB write already happened at q.\n\nSo yes: 1-byte OOB write of a byte from the packet, beyond the allocated buffer, when there's a mismatch of one between the number of separators counted and the number of dots actually inserted. This can happen when `dns_label_decode` follows a pointer to a location that is NOT a valid label start, so its returned length differs from what was counted.\n\nHmm, but the difference needs to be exactly enough. Let me think about the simplest mismatch: the uncompressed length counts `length += *p` for each real label and adds 1 per separator. dns_label_decode returns `*p` where p is the label (possibly after pointer). If the pointer points to a byte that is smaller than the real label, then the total copied bytes is less than slen, and p ends up < q, then `p != q` fails — no overflow, just under-fill. To get overflow, we need copied bytes + dots > slen.\n\nThe mismatch in the dot count: The loop writes a dot before each label except when `p == dst->vb_strvalue`. The uncompressed length adds a separator only when `!at_first_label`. at_first_label is true for the very first label processed. But if the first label is a pointer that jumps to a real label, then after following it, at_first_label stays true for that label (since the pointer branch doesn't set at_first_label=false; only the uncompressed-label branch does). Hmm wait, when we encounter a pointer, we set p=q and continue, without changing at_first_label. Then the real label is counted as first (no separator added). \n\nIn dns_label_decode, when current starts at the pointer, it follows to p and returns *p, and the loop writes no dot because p == vb_strvalue initially. Then current=next (pointer target + *p + 1). Now if next still < after, next iteration: writes a dot. Hmm.\n\nWhere can an extra dot appear? Consider: uncompressed_length counts labels starting from `start`. Suppose start is a pointer P1 → target T1 (real label A). It counts A (first, no sep). Then after A's bytes, if next is another pointer P2 → T2 (real label B), counts B with a separator. Total slen = len(A)+1+len(B). Now decode: current=start=P1. dns_label_decode follows P1→T1, returns len(A), next = T1+len(A)+1 = the byte after A which is P2. writes A (no dot since p==buf). p advances len(A). current=next=P2. Loop: current < after? after = next pointer from uncompressed_length = after B = T2+len(B)+1. P2 < after yes, *current = P2[0] = 0xc0 !=0. dns_label_decode follows P2→T2, returns len(B), next=T2+len(B)+1. writes '.' then B. p = len(A)+1+len(B) = q. Fine, no overflow.\n\nHmm. Now what if A and B overlap? Like T2 points into A. Then after following, the \"next\" computations could cause current < after longer, generating more dots than counted. Let's think: The counter (uncompressed_length) walks from start following pointers too, and tracks `next` only for the first label. It also uses lb (label tracking) to validate pointers point into previously-added blocks. But within a single call, dns_label_add is only called at the END. So during the walk, lb doesn't yet contain the labels of this name... wait, it does contain blocks from previously decoded names (in decode_record loop). And the first block is header 12..12. So a pointer is valid only if it points to a previously added label block (offset < block->end). \n\nHmm, so pointers must point to previously-decoded labels. In the decode_dns_labels loop, each label gets added to lb via fr_dns_label_uncompressed_length's final `dns_label_add(lb, start, *next)`. So earlier labels in the same name are added. \n\nSo an attacker can make the uncompressed_length walk follow pointer chains. Let me think of how to make the dot count mismatch.\n\nActually, let's reconsider. The key: uncompressed_length's loop condition is `while (p < end)`. It follows pointers and validates. Its length is sum of label lengths + separators. The decode loop condition is `while ((current < after) && (*current != 0))`. `after` = *next from uncompressed_length = the \"next label\" after the FIRST label. \n\nSo `after` is set to either p (after first label's terminator) or p+2 (after pointer) if the first label was a pointer.\n\nConsider: first label is a pointer P1 → T1, so after = P1+2. Then in decode loop: current=P1 < after, follow to T1, slen=len(T1), next=T1+len(T1)+1. write T1 bytes. current=next=T1+len+1. Now is current < after (=P1+2)? T1 is before P1 (backward pointer). T1+len+1 could be less than P1+2! Then loop continues and processes whatever bytes are at T1+len+1, which may be additional labels, generating extra dots and copies beyond what was counted!\n\nYes! That's the bug. Let me formalize.\n\nuncompressed_length walks: start=P1 (pointer to T1). It follows P1→T1. counts len(T1) as first label. Then continues from p=T1+len(T1)+1. It keeps walking until it hits 0x00 or end. So it counts ALL labels from T1 onward until terminator. length = len(T1) + (subsequent labels with separators) ... So slen includes everything from T1 to terminator. \n\nBut `after` is set to P1+2 (because first label was a pointer). The decode loop runs while current < after = P1+2. If T1 is located such that T1+len(T1)+1 < P1+2, then after processing T1, current = T1+len+1 which is < P1+2, so the loop continues! It processes bytes after T1 as if they were labels, even though uncompressed_length may have counted them as part of the same chain... Hmm, wait, uncompressed_length also walks through those bytes. So both walk the same bytes. The dot count could differ.\n\nLet's carefully compare.\n\nLet's denote the chain starting at T1: labels L1 (at T1), L2 (at T1+len1+1), ... until terminator. uncompressed_length counts: len1 (first, no sep) + for each subsequent Li: 1 + len_i. So total N = len1 + sum_{i>=2}(1+len_i).\n\nNow decode loop: current starts at P1. Iteration 1: follows P1→T1, slen=len1, writes no dot (p==buf), copies len1. current = T1+len1+1 (= start of L2).\nIteration 2: current < after(P1+2)? If yes, process L2: writes a dot, copies len2. current = start of L3.\n... continues as long as current < P1+2.\n\nThe decode loop stops when current >= P1+2 OR *current==0. So it processes labels while their start offset < P1+2. uncompressed_length processes ALL labels up to the terminator regardless. So decode loop might process FEWER labels than counted → under-fill → p<q → fail, no overflow. Hmm, that's the opposite.\n\nUnless... the terminator position. Hmm, let's think about a case where decode processes MORE.\n\nActually, note that dns_label_decode's `next = p + *p + 1` where p is the label. It does NOT check for terminator bytes along the way; it just skips. Whereas uncompressed_length checks each byte at label start for 0x00 and stops. And when following pointers, dns_label_decode does NOT recursively follow; it only follows ONE pointer then treats p as a real label. uncompressed_length follows pointers in a loop (chain). \n\nSo if a label position in the decode chain contains a pointer, dns_label_decode follows it and returns the target's length, while uncompressed_length follows the pointer and continues walking from the target. These should be similar but the `after` boundary differs.\n\nHmm, let me think about making decode write more than slen. The overflow happens if at some iteration p == q-1 and we write a dot + 1 byte. That requires the number of (dots + bytes) to exceed slen by at least 1 at some point. \n\nAlternative: make `slen` returned by dns_label_decode LARGER than the actual counted label at that position. Since buffer size was computed by uncompressed_length following the chain, and dns_label_decode follows pointers differently (only one level, or follows a pointer that uncompressed_length might have treated differently)...\n\nKey difference: uncompressed_length follows pointer chains: pointer → possibly another pointer? No wait, it checks `if (*q > 63)` → error \"does not point to the start of a label\" if the target is itself a pointer. So pointers can't point to pointers. It only follows one level too, but in a loop (each pointer followed once, target must be a real label, then continues after target).\n\ndns_label_decode: if *p > 63 (pointer), follow once to target, return *target as length. So same one-level follow.\n\nHmm so where's the discrepancy? Consider the boundary `after`. In uncompressed_length, `after` (next) is set:\n- if the first label is a real label ending with 0x00 at position k: *next = k+1.\n- if first label is a pointer: *next = p+2 (after the pointer).\n- The pointer branch sets `already_set_next=true` only `if (current == start)`, i.e. only for the first pointer encountered while current==start.\n\nNow decode loop runs `while current < after`. If after is small (pointer case, P1+2), and T1 is located BEFORE P1, then after processing L1 at T1, current = start of L2 which might still be < P1+2, so it processes L2. But uncompressed_length counted L2 too. So still matched.\n\nHold on. Let me reconsider: uncompressed_length's walk. When it follows P1→T1, then continues from T1. But wait — does it continue from T1+len1+1, or does it re-examine? It sets p = current = q (T1) then `continue`. Loop: p=T1. It's a real label (assuming *T1 <= 63 and != 0). counts len1. p += len1+1 → start of L2. Continues. Yes.\n\nSo both walk from T1 onward. The only difference is the stopping condition. uncompressed_length stops at terminator or end. decode loop stops at `current >= after` or `*current == 0`. \n\nIf after = P1+2 is LARGE (P1 is late in the buffer), decode loop continues far past the terminator? No — it checks *current == 0, so it stops at terminator too. Hmm. But dns_label_decode skips bytes: next = p + *p + 1. If the byte at some position is a small nonzero value that isn't the terminator... it's treated as a label length and skipped. uncompressed_length would treat the same. \n\nWait, actually the critical thing: uncompressed_length processes labels in order and validates characters, and stops at a 0x00 at a label-start position. dns_label_decode does not check whether the \"label\" position after following